---
import BaseLayout from "../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";

const page = {
  title: "What a Bird DNA Test Report Should Contain | SENO",
  h1: "What a Bird DNA Test Report Should Contain",
  description: "How to read a bird DNA test report as an auditor: sample identity, method, cutoff, controls, limitations, dates, amendments, and pre-acceptance checks.",
  canonical: "/bird-dna-test-report/",
  author: "SENO Avian Genetic Test",
  date: "November 22, 2026",
};
const articleHtml = `
<p>A breeder receives a one-page PDF with a bird name, a photograph and the word "positive." The document looks official. It does not say which sample was tested, which target was detected, what cutoff was used or whether controls passed. The result may be real, but the report cannot carry the decision by itself. The <a href="/avian-testing-laboratory-quality-validation/">laboratory quality and validation guide</a> treats reporting as the last controlled step in the evidence chain.</p>
<p>Most clients keep the report and discard the emails, plate sheets and sample forms. That makes the report the durable record of what was done. Read every field as an auditor would, beginning with the result line but continuing through the evidence that supports it.</p>

<h2>The Result Line Is the Shortest Part</h2>
<p>A result has meaning only with its identifiers, method, decision rule and validity statement. "Detected" is incomplete if the target is unnamed. "Negative" is not reassuring if the assay failed or the sample lacked amplifiable DNA. "Inconclusive" concerns the strength of evidence.</p>
<p>The report should let an independent reader reconstruct what was requested, what was tested and how the call was made. A missing field cannot be inferred from the layout or logo.</p>

<h2>Client, Sample, and Test Identity</h2>
<p>Start with the link between document and physical sample. The report should carry a client or case identifier, unique sample identifier, submitted species, sample type and receipt date. A bird's name should be secondary to the unique key; "Blue" alone cannot distinguish two birds.</p>
<p>The test name needs enough detail to show intended use. "Avian DNA" is too broad. A usable entry names the question, such as sex determination, parentage or detection of a named agent, plus the supported species.</p>
<div class="table-wrap">
<table class="data-table">
<thead>
<tr><th>Report element</th><th>Why it matters</th><th>What to check</th></tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Client and sample identifiers</td><td>Links result to the submitted bird or case</td><td>Unique key, species, sample type, receipt date</td></tr>
<tr><td>Test name and intended use</td><td>Defines the question answered</td><td>Specific test, purpose, supported matrix</td></tr>
<tr><td>Method and target</td><td>Shows what was measured and how</td><td>Assay, target, platform and extraction</td></tr>
<tr><td>Result and decision rule</td><td>Explains how the call was reached</td><td>Wording, cutoff and inconclusive category</td></tr>
<tr><td>Control and validity statement</td><td>Shows whether the run was acceptable</td><td>Controls run, outcome and invalid-run rule</td></tr>
<tr><td>Dates and laboratory identity</td><td>Places the report in time and names its issuer</td><td>Receipt, testing, authorization and issue dates</td></tr>
<tr><td>Version and amendments</td><td>Prevents an old report being treated as current</td><td>Version, pages and amendment history</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</div>

<h2>Method, Target, and Result Wording</h2>
<p>The method section should name the analytical approach, target and extraction route without disclosing details that would invalidate the test. It should state whether the test is qualitative or quantitative and give reporting categories. A citation supports the design, but not correct use on this sample.</p>
<p>Result wording should match the intended use and validated decision rule. "Detected" describes target evidence, not a diagnosis. "Not detected" means the target was not found within assay limits; it does not prove the bird is free of every agent or variant. "Positive" should name the target and cutoff.</p>
<div class="table-wrap">
<table class="data-table">
<thead>
<tr><th>Result wording</th><th>Meaning</th><th>What it does not mean</th></tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Detected</td><td>Target signal met reporting criteria</td><td>Disease, infectiousness or severity</td></tr>
<tr><td>Not detected</td><td>No target signal met criteria</td><td>Guaranteed absence after poor sampling</td></tr>
<tr><td>Positive</td><td>A named target was found</td><td>A complete diagnosis or proof of spread</td></tr>
<tr><td>Negative</td><td>Target evidence was below threshold</td><td>A valid run when controls failed</td></tr>
<tr><td>Invalid</td><td>The run did not meet acceptance criteria</td><td>A negative or inconclusive biological result</td></tr>
<tr><td>Inconclusive</td><td>Evidence was insufficient or conflicting</td><td>Permission to reinterpret it as negative</td></tr>
<tr><td>Insufficient signal</td><td>Recovery or amplification fell below the limit</td><td>A finding about the bird's status</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</div>

<h2>Interpretation, Limitations, and Cutoffs</h2>
<p>A report should state its limits in plain language: sample type, species coverage, target variants, detection threshold and interference. For parentage, it should state the markers and exclusion logic. For sexing, it should state the species limitation and what an inconclusive result means.</p>
<p>Not every qualitative test has a numeric reference range, but each needs a decision rule. Ask whether a cutoff, cycle threshold, signal-to-noise rule or repeat criterion determined the call. A number without an acceptance range proves little.</p>

<h2>Controls, Validity, and Dates</h2>
<p>The report should show that the batch was accepted before the sample result was released. It may summarize control values, but should name the control categories and state their outcome. If a control failed and the sample was repeated, the final report should identify the accepted run and preserve the original record.</p>
<p>Dates should distinguish receipt, testing, review and issue. One date for everything hides delays and makes a repeat hard to audit. The laboratory's identity, site, authorizing person and contact details belong on the report.</p>

<h2>Identity, Integrity, and Amendments</h2>
<p>Check the page count, report version and amendment note. A revised report should say what changed, why, who authorized it and which version it replaces. It should not silently replace a result. Confirm that numbered pages form a complete document and signatures can be attributed to the laboratory.</p>
<p>Raw data are not always sent to clients, but the laboratory should link them to the accession, extraction, plate and report. Ask about the retention period and how a correction is traced. The <a href="/sample-traceability-chain-of-custody-dna-testing/">sample traceability guide</a> explains the chain behind that link, while the <a href="/failed-avian-dna-tests/">failed-test guide</a> covers what a failure notice should add. The <a href="https://data.aviantestpro.com/quality-control/lab-workflow/">quality-control workflow reference</a> shows the records a report should summarize.</p>

<h2>What a Report Should Not Claim and a Pre-Acceptance Checklist</h2>
<p>A report should not imply that a test diagnoses disease, guarantees freedom from infection, establishes identity without a custody trail, or predicts performance. It should not use an accreditation logo to imply that every menu test is covered, call a weak extraction negative, or omit a decision-changing limitation.</p>
<p>Before accepting a report, check six things: the sample identifier matches the submission; the test and target match the request; the result category is defined; the validity statement is present; the date and authorization are clear; and the limitations fit your use. If any item is missing, request a corrected report. The <a href="/bird-dna-test-accuracy-validation/">accuracy validation guide</a> helps interpret performance claims, and the <a href="/contact/">laboratory contact route</a> can be used to request missing fields.</p>

<h2>Scenario: The One-Line Positive PDF</h2>
<p>A breeder receives a PDF that says only "Positive" above a photograph of a parrot. It has no case number, sample identifier, target, method, cutoff, control statement, laboratory address, authorizing person or issue date. The breeder wants to use it in a sale dispute.</p>
<p>The first question is not whether the result is true; it is what the document can establish. Without a sample identifier, the report cannot show which bird was tested. Without a target, "positive" does not identify the agent or trait. Without a method and cutoff, the reader cannot tell what signal generated the call. Without controls, there is no evidence that the batch was accepted. The photograph does not repair those gaps. They determine whether the result is usable in a dispute or a breeding decision.</p>
<p>Request the full report and template. Ask for the accession, species, sample type, test and intended use, target and method, result criteria, control outcome, issue and authorization details, page count and amendment history. Ask whether raw data are linked to the accession. If the laboratory cannot produce those fields, treat the PDF as a preliminary message, not a durable result.</p>
<p>A defensible dispute usually needs a new sample collected under a documented identity process and tested with a report that states its limits. A retest of material with no accession trail may confirm a signal but cannot show that it came from the disputed bird. The one-line PDF may prompt further testing, but it is not proof of the original claim.</p>

<h2>What we would do</h2>
<p>We would compare the report to the submission form and request the missing identifiers, method, decision rule, control outcome, issue date and authorization before accepting the result. We would ask for the amendment history and the record linking the data to the sample. The exception is a preliminary message clearly labelled as provisional; then we would treat it as a notification and wait for the final report before making a decision.</p>

<h2>Questions buyers ask</h2>
<h3>What is the minimum information in a bird DNA test report?</h3>
<p>At least a unique sample identifier, test name and intended use, method or target, result category, validity statement, laboratory identity and issue date. A disputed report also needs page control, authorization and amendment history.</p>
<h3>Why does the sample identifier matter more than the bird's name?</h3>
<p>Names can repeat, change spelling or refer to several birds in one loft. A unique accession key lets the laboratory and client trace the result to the collection record and detect a swap. The name is additional context.</p>
<h3>What does positive mean without a stated cutoff?</h3>
<p>It usually means some target signal met an internal rule, but the reader cannot judge the strength or specificity of that evidence. Ask for the target, method, criteria and control outcome before treating it as a finding.</p>
<h3>Can I rely on a report that gives no method?</h3>
<p>You can note the claimed result, but cannot verify what was measured or whether the method suits your species and sample. Treat it as incomplete evidence and request a method summary and limitations.</p>
<h3>Should the laboratory send raw data with every report?</h3>
<p>Not necessarily. Many laboratories retain raw files and issue a summary. Ask how long data are kept and how they link to the sample. For a dispute, request records under policy; the PDF may not be the whole file.</p>

<h2>Sources</h2>
<ol>
<li><a href="https://www.iso.org/ISO-IEC-17025-testing-and-calibration-laboratories.html">ISO/IEC 17025: testing and calibration laboratories</a>, International Organization for Standardization.</li>
<li><a href="https://www.iso.org/standard/76677.html">ISO 15189:2022, Medical laboratories</a>, International Organization for Standardization.</li>
<li><a href="https://doi.org/10.1373/clinchem.2008.112797">MIQE guidelines for quantitative real-time PCR experiments</a>, Clinical Chemistry.</li>
<li><a href="https://www.aavld.org/accreditation-requirements">Accreditation requirements</a>, American Association of Veterinary Laboratory Diagnosticians.</li>
<li><a href="https://data.aviantestpro.com/quality-control/lab-workflow/">Laboratory quality-control workflow</a>, SENO technical data.</li>
</ol>
`;
